WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.276
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.010
SAMI ZUHURUDDIN: Good afternoon.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.200
My name is Sami Zuhuruddin.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.910
I'm a staff solution architect
on the Cloud platform team.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:18.320
I'm going to be talking to you
today about cloud forensics.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.500
Cloud forensics machine learning
server, this DevOps quantum

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.680
computing forensics edge--

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.150
no, that would be a great talk.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.210
That's like everything
I could throw in there.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.220
No, this talk is
called Forensics 101

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.370
and I hope by the title
that sort of clued folks in.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.084
The 101 is we're starting
with the fundamentals,

00:00:37.084 --> 00:00:38.750
we're starting with
the basics, and then

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.130
from there, we're advancing.

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:44.444
We wouldn't be in much shape
talking about advanced subjects

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:45.860
if we weren't
covering the basics,

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.651
so that's definitely
kind of the level set.

00:00:47.651 --> 00:00:50.030
We're going to talk about
techniques, practices,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.190
things you need to do
for your environment,

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.880
and hopefully leave you with
a lot of practical things

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:57.530
that you can do.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:00.110
This is not necessarily
a talk about security.

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:02.360
We do a lot to secure
our environments

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.099
and perfect security is a goal.

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:06.440
Perfect security should
be the outcome or the goal

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.120
of any security program.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.100
But we live in a world
where infiltrations happen.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.220
That's just,
unfortunately, the reality.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.320
We hear about them, and
when we hear about them,

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:16.070
they're not great.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:17.528
Sometimes we don't
hear about them.

00:01:17.528 --> 00:01:19.190
They're taken care
of internally.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:22.220
But when we do hear about them,
they've been, in some cases--

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.820
you know, it's not uncommon
to hear months or years that

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.890
something has been
allowed to sit there--

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.400
some bad actor has been allowed
to act in our environment.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.240
And they're up to no good.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.150
They're up to stealing from us.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.505
It could be stealing
sensitive customer data.

00:01:37.505 --> 00:01:41.600
It could be stealing
our trade secrets.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.050
It could be stealing literally
half a penny at a time.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.300
That would make a great movie.

00:01:49.744 --> 00:01:52.160
But maybe it's not a half a
penny, maybe it's half a coin.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.152
In this case, maybe
it's half a bitcoin.

00:01:54.152 --> 00:01:55.610
That's not an
uncommon application,

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.250
where they're literally stealing
your computing resources.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.605
But regardless, they're
in our infrastructure.

00:02:00.605 --> 00:02:02.480
And if we turn it into
an engineering problem

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.860
for a second, our goal
is to optimize the time

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.990
from which they
have possibly landed

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.120
on our infrastructure
or our environment

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.405
to the time we were able to
detect and get rid of it.

00:02:14.405 --> 00:02:18.560
We want to optimize to
minimize dwell time.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.050
We want to get-- you know,
from the time they figure out

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.700
how to create an
exploit to the time

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.491
that we're able to do
something about that,

00:02:24.491 --> 00:02:27.380
we want that to be as
little as possible,

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:31.040
because the longer we
allow that to fester,

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.380
that's money potentially lost.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.170
So what are the
objectives for today?

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:40.927
What also amazes me is
that, yes, infiltrators

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:42.760
are making their way
into our infrastructure

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.214
despite security
measures, but it's also,

00:02:45.214 --> 00:02:47.380
when these stories come out
about how things happen,

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.065
it's fascinating to watch
the process and progression

00:02:50.065 --> 00:02:52.565
that professionals
in that space,

00:02:52.565 --> 00:02:55.240
because they are looking at
how to uncover the bad guys,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.620
the process they go through.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:58.328
And kind of one of
the fundamental pieces

00:02:58.328 --> 00:03:00.050
is that it's not magic.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:01.510
It's forensic.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:04.750
They're looking at data, much
like any other profession.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.570
They're looking at
pieces of evidence

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:10.030
that allow them to formulate
a certain pattern of behavior

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.029
and then uncover things.

00:03:11.029 --> 00:03:14.467
So today's activity largely
focuses around that activity.

00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:16.300
We're going to talk
about a number of things

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.550
we need to be doing
in our environments

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:19.867
to help that process along.

00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:21.700
We're going to go over
the tools explicitly.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.050
This is kind of
the highest level

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:24.300
that we'll get to in the talk.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.610
The rest of the talk will talk
about tools and configurations

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.546
of tools and things
that we can do.

00:03:30.546 --> 00:03:32.170
Also, at the end,
we'll sort of wrap up

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:37.300
with an example of what a whole
forensics pipeline looks like.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.849
This is Cloud and
so it wouldn't be

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:40.390
complete without
talking about things

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:46.150
we can do in an automated
fashion to really sort of make

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:52.512
our whole forensics activities
within our environments

00:03:52.512 --> 00:03:53.470
a little more seamless.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.440
All right.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.660
So standard disclaimer.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.550
Going through your
environments and looking

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.740
for what a bad actor
may be doing and try

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.614
to uncover it and try to get
it out of your environment.

00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:08.530
It's serious business.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.870
It shouldn't be taken lightly.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.290
There is tradecraft
out there for those

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.140
who are trained to look
at incident response

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:20.730
to find a bad guy within
your infrastructure

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.352
and it's our job to help them.

00:04:23.352 --> 00:04:24.810
It's our job to
really put together

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:27.540
a very concise and
very well-curated

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.210
set of information that allows
those individuals-- if you're

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:33.420
here, that's great, but allows
those individuals to minimize

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.270
the amount of time that
something bad is inside

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.280
of our environment.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.120
And I kind of analogize this
to being medically informed

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.460
and making sort of
the good choices

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.060
when it comes to sort of
your diet or your exercise

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.150
or things, you can get away
with googling a bunch of stuff

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:53.640
to live really a good life,
but at some point or another,

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.250
you have to decide, you know,
it's time to call in a doctor.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.650
It's time to sort of step it up.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:59.670
And that's the way I'd encourage
you to look at the information

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:00.810
we're going to present here.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:02.268
It is pretty handy
and it does sort

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:04.830
of open your eyes
to what's available

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:07.860
but try not to be over-cavalier
and know when to bring

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.292
in the right resources.

00:05:12.361 --> 00:05:12.860
All right.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:14.693
So we're going to walk
through these stages.

00:05:14.693 --> 00:05:17.800
These are sort of
different stages of--

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:21.290
or categories of activities
that we want to walk through

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:25.430
in the process of helping
prepare our environment

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:27.140
for dealing with an incident.

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:29.960
If an incident happens, we
want to be as well-prepared

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:30.489
as we can.

00:05:30.489 --> 00:05:31.280
It's going to suck.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.180
It's not something we
want to go through.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.290
But we can either be
extremely poorly prepared,

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:38.600
in which case we just
don't know where to start--

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.630
we're literally starting
at square 0, square 1--

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:43.952
or we can be in a
much better position.

00:05:43.952 --> 00:05:45.410
And that's our
objective here today

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:46.919
is to be in a much
better position.

00:05:46.919 --> 00:05:48.710
And generally, we sort
of move to the right

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:52.015
of this orchestration of
events, but our activities

00:05:52.015 --> 00:05:53.390
are going to fall
in this bucket.

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:56.540
And today, just to sort of,
you know, in full transparency,

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:59.000
today we're really going to
be talking about collection.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.860
This is really how
we boost our position

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.290
and give an analyst
or somebody that's

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:06.434
investigating an incident
the best ammunition to sort

00:06:06.434 --> 00:06:07.100
of move forward.

00:06:14.887 --> 00:06:16.970
I said we're going to talk
about collection mainly

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:18.830
but it wouldn't be
complete without talking

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:23.610
about prevention,
and by that, I mean

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:27.450
these are the kinds of
activities that we go through

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:31.030
in securing our infrastructure.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:33.120
And in a high level,
you could think of it

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.820
as the three-step
sort of processes.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:39.180
Secure your perimeter, go
through defense in depth,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.890
and then monitor how you're
doing at any given point.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:46.350
But the reason this
is important-- this

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.060
is sort of a symbiotic talk.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:49.800
I mean, there's so
many talks regarding

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.910
securing your infrastructure
and how to properly lock things

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.677
down, but insofar as when
we talk about it in today's

00:06:56.677 --> 00:06:59.010
context or in the context of
forensics, the reason we do

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:02.700
this is, on its own value,
it's incredibly needed

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.067
within our environments,
but if we don't do it,

00:07:05.067 --> 00:07:07.650
we're going to generate a bunch
of noise-- a bunch of garbage.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.441
And this is sort of
the adage of, you know,

00:07:09.441 --> 00:07:11.320
garbage in, garbage out.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:13.550
If we've left everything
wide open to the internet

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:15.420
and we're just being
bombarded with attacks,

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:18.060
we don't want to float through
a bunch of needless information

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:21.240
to our downstream processes
or our downstream analyses.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.180
We need to take care of making
sure things like our software

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.220
pedigree-- if we are
building software

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:28.830
and we're sourcing components
from different locations,

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:30.930
are we keeping up
with the security

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.380
bulletins or the vulnerability
bulletins with those libraries?

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:35.860
If we're not, then
that's something

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.190
obviously an attacker is
going to find and leverage

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.290
and say, oh, they're
using unpatched version x.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:43.620
We can use a well-known exploit.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:46.420
So these are activities that
absolutely must be done,

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:49.350
and if you don't
do it, then it's

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:51.480
not only just sort of
weakening your environment.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.470
It's going to make
it so that we're

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:56.520
going to end up crying
wolf a bunch of times

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.520
and making it really
difficult for a professional

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.412
to come in and properly
analyze the environment.

00:08:02.412 --> 00:08:03.870
And by the way, I
mean, not only is

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:07.860
it time is money in terms
of how quickly we uncover

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:11.040
where a bad actor has planted
something in our environment.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.374
These resources generally
that are doing the analysis,

00:08:13.374 --> 00:08:14.040
they cost money.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:15.581
You want them to
come in and you want

00:08:15.581 --> 00:08:17.760
them to sort of not have
to waste a lot of time

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:22.110
on finding the needle
in the haystack.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:28.430
So a great Englishman
once said, who

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:31.667
here likes audience
participation?

00:08:31.667 --> 00:08:33.500
Let's organize into
groups of 20-- no, let's

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:36.330
not organize into groups of 20.

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:37.260
Let's call it out.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:40.370
What are some things
that sort of cause one

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:43.220
to think that they have
an incident going on

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.470
or somebody has found a way
into their infrastructure?

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:46.261
Go ahead.

00:08:46.261 --> 00:08:49.030
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:52.370
SAMI ZUHURUDDIN:
Spiked resource usage.

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:53.390
Maybe the-- go ahead.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:55.170
AUDIENCE: Anomalous
network traffic.

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:58.380
SAMI ZUHURUDDIN: Anomalous
network traffic, serverless.

00:08:58.380 --> 00:08:59.220
Yeah, go ahead.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:00.120
AUDIENCE: Blackmail.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:00.870
SAMI ZUHURUDDIN: Blackmail.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:01.420
Ransom notes.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.003
So there's a lot of
things that go on.

00:09:03.003 --> 00:09:06.580
The point is, all those people
are probably in the craft--

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:08.220
and the point is,
everybody should

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:10.380
have an incident response plan.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:12.157
If you don't, look around.

00:09:12.157 --> 00:09:13.740
It sounds like there's
about 400 of us

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.865
here that might be
able to help out.

00:09:15.865 --> 00:09:17.490
But we should have
an incident response

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.310
plan to know what to do.

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:26.135
It could be literally
some authority or some law

00:09:26.135 --> 00:09:27.510
enforcement that's
come and said,

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:29.610
we have reason to believe that
you're sending a lot of data

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.380
to places you shouldn't
be sending data.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:33.780
So whatever it is, think
about yourself in that moment.

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:35.280
And what are you going
to do if somebody says,

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:36.600
you know, there is strong
evidence here that you're

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:37.794
going to be compromised?

00:09:37.794 --> 00:09:39.210
So this is in that
list of to-dos.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:41.280
I'm going to give you a
couple of not-to-dos, OK?

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:48.801
First one is do not simply
say, oh my gosh, I'm panicked.

00:09:48.801 --> 00:09:49.800
I don't know what to do.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:50.970
Somebody is in my
infrastructure.

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:51.810
I know what to do.

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:52.710
This is Cloud.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.830
I'll hit Delete.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:58.720
We don't want to do this
for a couple of reasons.

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:02.430
Number one is it just completely
obliterates the forensic trail

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:03.540
that you want to follow.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.340
But in the chess match of-- if
you think about it that way--

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:08.400
if you think about somebody
trying to figure out

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.730
how to take advantage
of your infrastructure,

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:13.830
you've made a very naive move.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:17.040
You've told your opponent
that you really maybe

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.059
don't have a game plan.

00:10:18.059 --> 00:10:19.350
You haven't figured things out.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.299
And if they were
unaggressive at that point,

00:10:21.299 --> 00:10:23.340
they're going to get highly
aggressive after they

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:24.423
see that you're onto them.

00:10:24.423 --> 00:10:26.970
Maybe they're going to step
up exfiltrating all your data.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:28.150
Maybe they're going to
step up their attack.

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:30.649
Maybe they're going to lock you
out of their infrastructure.

00:10:30.649 --> 00:10:33.280
So you will obviously
terminate your infrastructure,

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:35.230
but do it at the right time.

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:38.250
Don't make a naive move that
accelerates things needlessly.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.320
The key point is, take a small
breath, say, I have a plan.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:42.550
I know what to do.

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:45.420
I'm not going to give away my
hand and reveal to an attacker

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.712
that I know they're
in my infrastructure.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:51.450
Don't be a hero.

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:57.510
Do not grab-- don't just,
you know, we're infiltrated.

00:10:57.510 --> 00:10:58.350
I know what to do.

00:10:58.350 --> 00:10:59.670
I got this.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:04.495
I'll just log into the server
using SSH so it's secure.

00:11:04.495 --> 00:11:06.870
Realize, when there is a
machine that's been compromised,

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:08.328
that means somebody
has figured out

00:11:08.328 --> 00:11:09.495
how to take advantage of it.

00:11:09.495 --> 00:11:11.077
And so in some form
or another, if you

00:11:11.077 --> 00:11:13.110
log into a machine that's
knowingly compromised,

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.840
you are in some way handing
over credentials to an attacker.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.549
So don't have the
inclination to do that.

00:11:17.549 --> 00:11:19.173
Now these two things
are very important

00:11:19.173 --> 00:11:21.780
because the rest of this talk
is going to be largely centered

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:22.939
around, so what do I do?

00:11:22.939 --> 00:11:24.730
You know, how do I get
around these things?

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:29.220
I do want to know what's going
on in these environments.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:32.640
But generally speaking,
don't log in and then

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.910
sort of say, you know, I know a
thing or two about top and grep

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:37.890
and ps, and I can look at logs.

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:39.510
It's not our general plan.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:44.230
So the bulk of today.

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:45.970
The bulk of today is
collecting things.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.370
Like we said, resources
and the professionals that

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:52.147
are going to come in to
do this work for you,

00:11:52.147 --> 00:11:54.730
you could either say, I have no
idea what's in my environment.

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:55.561
Please take a look.

00:11:55.561 --> 00:11:56.560
Here are my credentials.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:57.139
Go ahead.

00:11:57.139 --> 00:11:58.930
And they're going to
start from square one.

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:00.790
Not only does that cost
you time and money,

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:02.414
it's just not an
efficient use of time.

00:12:02.414 --> 00:12:04.704
We're trying to get folks
out of our infrastructure.

00:12:04.704 --> 00:12:06.370
So we're going to go
through collection.

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:09.400
We want, as part of our
preparation process, things

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:11.980
that we're going to
have done ahead of time

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:13.780
is to be collecting
a lot of information

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:17.719
so that when something
happens, we're going to say,

00:12:17.719 --> 00:12:19.510
yes, you're finding a
needle in a haystack,

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:22.090
but we've really curated
the haystack down

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:24.022
to make it a very
small bundle of things.

00:12:24.022 --> 00:12:25.480
We think there's
something in here.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:27.440
We think what you're
going to find is in here.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.210
Please take a look.

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:31.390
And it's generally
these three buckets--

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:35.616
logs, disks, and
live information.

00:12:35.616 --> 00:12:37.240
Live information
because more and more,

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:38.890
we're not in a
situation where we're

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:43.200
dealing with infected or
compromised hosts that

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.910
are sitting under our desks or
sitting inside of our office.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:47.680
We're in Cloud.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:50.729
So in Cloud, you don't
have that liberty, right?

00:12:50.729 --> 00:12:53.020
You have to think about how
to do things over the wire,

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:55.190
over the internet.

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:58.660
And so, more and more, we
need tools and mechanisms

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:01.742
to connect to things
while they're online,

00:13:01.742 --> 00:13:02.950
while they're on the network.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:05.533
So generally we're going to go
through activities for the rest

00:13:05.533 --> 00:13:08.200
of this talk that are talking
about how to collect logs,

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:11.080
what to do about getting
disks-- disks are obviously one

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.350
of the most central
pieces of information

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:15.430
in an analyst's job--

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:19.000
and then how to collect
information real-time,

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:22.620
live over the network.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:25.770
Before we collect
anything, though, we

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:28.390
want to put things into
a secured location.

00:13:28.390 --> 00:13:32.430
We don't want to collect all
these buckets of information

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:34.530
and then have that
bucket be a leaky bucket.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:36.960
We want to basically
put them into a vault.

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:39.340
And that vault-- if you think
about the Google project

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:42.210
organization, you're
going to have projects.

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:44.624
Your projects are where
your services are running,

00:13:44.624 --> 00:13:46.290
things that are talking
to the internet,

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:49.699
and this is where your, quote
unquote, "compromised" activity

00:13:49.699 --> 00:13:50.490
might be happening.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:53.031
But on the other side of that,
your security apparatus within

00:13:53.031 --> 00:13:56.670
your organization, those who
are responsible for collecting

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:59.844
these artifacts, they should
have their own highly isolated

00:13:59.844 --> 00:14:01.260
environment-- a
separate project--

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:04.680
and that's designated
for artifacts.

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:07.840
And it's a one-way
flow of information

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:10.560
and we're going to set it up
so that the only folks that

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:15.060
have access to that is
a highly sort of refined

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:16.590
list of individuals.

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:19.770
This isn't something even that
most people should know exist.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.230
But set this up
first so that we have

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:23.782
a destination for everything.

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:29.940
So logs was the first
thing in our agenda.

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:31.427
Logs is a broad category.

00:14:31.427 --> 00:14:33.260
Anybody that's had to
troubleshoot a problem

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:37.220
knows that logs is
your best asset.

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:40.190
And within Cloud
platform, there's

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:41.720
sort of two camps of logs.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:44.780
There's logs that
we generate as part

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:46.790
of Google that
tell you everything

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:47.910
about your environment.

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:50.270
And these are admin
activity logs,

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:54.890
so who launched an instance,
who modified a firewall rule,

00:14:54.890 --> 00:14:55.820
and data access.

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:58.610
So if you're using any of our
data products, like BigQuery,

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:00.860
and you're storing all your
customer data on BigQuery,

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:02.660
well, that becomes
a tangible asset

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:05.450
that's a vector for somebody
to try to come in and attack.

00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:09.830
So we can log activities
regarding your data access.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:10.830
So we take care of that.

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:13.542
These are
platform-generated logs.

00:15:13.542 --> 00:15:15.000
The other side of
the fence is logs

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.830
that you generate-- logs that
your application generates.

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:19.800
And so we're going to
talk about basically

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:23.460
each one of these
individually and where you

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:27.680
should destine these things.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:29.740
So the nice thing is that,
within Cloud platform,

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:31.698
there is a facility called
Stackdriver Logging.

00:15:31.698 --> 00:15:35.830
This is how we surface all
log information to you.

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:38.620
But it's not simply for the
platform-generated logs.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:40.980
It's a full-fledged
logging service.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:43.490
And this is where you would
instrument and say, hey,

00:15:43.490 --> 00:15:46.130
I'm going to be generating
logs from my instances.

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:48.845
I'm going to be generating
logs from my application.

00:15:48.845 --> 00:15:50.470
Those are all going
to basically end up

00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:52.910
in the same monolithic point.

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:58.110
Now if you have another logging
instrument, that's fine,

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:00.110
but the point is, you
should be sending all logs

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:03.080
to some centralized place that
allows you to pivot and look

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:03.710
at it.

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:05.270
Now the platform-generated
logs, like I said,

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:07.019
those are just given
to you automatically.

00:16:07.019 --> 00:16:10.119
They make their way into
Stackdriver Logging.

00:16:10.119 --> 00:16:11.660
But you have to do
a couple of things

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:13.670
in order to get user
logs and application

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:18.004
logs into that environment.

00:16:18.004 --> 00:16:20.420
So we'll talk about it as three
phases, ingest, filtering,

00:16:20.420 --> 00:16:21.710
and export.

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:23.100
So ingest is really simple.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:24.980
It's actually a very
frictionless thing.

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:26.965
When you're creating
your VMs or when

00:16:26.965 --> 00:16:28.340
you're creating
your environment,

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:30.381
make sure to install a
Stackdriver Logging agent.

00:16:30.381 --> 00:16:33.140
It's the logging agent that's
essentially a packaged Fluentd

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:33.789
agent.

00:16:33.789 --> 00:16:35.330
And that's really
all you have to do.

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:38.630
It's preconfigured to look at
all the standard normal places

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:40.530
within your operating system--

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:43.220
your syslog and
other activities.

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:45.170
And as soon as it
turns on, it's going

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:47.390
to be able to use
instance information,

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:49.880
authenticate, and send it to
the Stackdriver Logging API.

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:54.320
You can do this from AWS as well
and install the logging agent

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:56.960
on your EC2 instances and
it will go effectively

00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:59.270
to one destination.

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:01.700
In your application
code, we have

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:04.099
support for several
different languages

00:17:04.099 --> 00:17:05.780
essentially in your
application code.

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:08.304
Instead of one approach would
maybe just to be logging out

00:17:08.304 --> 00:17:09.470
to the system level logging.

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:12.011
The other is, your application
code directly could instrument

00:17:12.011 --> 00:17:13.369
talking to the Stackdriver API.

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:15.785
And so you've got the sort of
async process, which is just

00:17:15.785 --> 00:17:17.242
sending out log information.

00:17:17.242 --> 00:17:18.950
If you're running in
serverless or you're

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:20.569
running in an environment
where you can't necessarily

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:22.400
rely on collecting
the system-level logs,

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:25.140
you want to emit those logs
directly from your application,

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:26.723
then you would simply
turn around-- we

00:17:26.723 --> 00:17:29.540
all left a lot of
samples on how to include

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:32.440
logging capabilities within
your application co-directly.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:37.610
And those don't necessarily
need to go through the operating

00:17:37.610 --> 00:17:39.707
system.

00:17:39.707 --> 00:17:41.790
Once it's in Stackdriver
Logging-- and Stackdriver

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:44.970
Logging, I'm not making sort
of any claims about sort

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:47.250
of the volume of logs and
the cost of logs and things

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:47.749
like that.

00:17:47.749 --> 00:17:50.460
That's obviously something you
should estimate and figure out

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:53.310
how long data should sit
there and where it should move

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:54.420
and how much it will cost.

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:56.855
But the nice thing is, once
it's in Stackdriver Logging,

00:17:56.855 --> 00:17:58.980
by and large, you get this
sort of freeform search.

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:01.620
You can do advanced filters,
you can do custom filters,

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.390
you can look at
different things--

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:06.240
like if you wanted to know
why a particular server is out

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:07.800
of sync, you could
run it and simply

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:11.070
look for what the
NTP daemon was doing

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:15.240
on a particular instance or all
instances at a certain time.

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:18.340
And this is going to be really
instrumental later in the talk

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:20.790
where we show you can do some
fancy things with filtering

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:24.967
that will tell you, hey, there's
something fishy going on.

00:18:24.967 --> 00:18:26.800
But most likely, this
is sort of a pit stop.

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:28.200
Stackdriver Logging
is a pit stop

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.155
on the way to
something different.

00:18:30.155 --> 00:18:31.280
We want to save these logs.

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:34.290
Like I said, the order of
magnitude on these attacks

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:37.230
is not a couple
of days or weeks.

00:18:37.230 --> 00:18:39.570
Sometimes we're finding out
they're months or years.

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:43.890
And so we want a destination to
send these logs to long-term.

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:46.860
Stackdriver Logging
gives you three outlets

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:49.260
to send your information out to.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.540
BigQuery, which is kind
of like the choice I

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:54.540
would sort of default here, the
logs are already structured.

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:57.732
That lends very nicely to
export them to BigQuery, where

00:18:57.732 --> 00:18:59.940
they will already be searchable
and they will already

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:02.280
be able to query against.

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:04.350
But then, if you ever
wanted them out of BigQuery

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:06.683
in, say, a flat file format,
you can always do an Unload

00:19:06.683 --> 00:19:08.430
operation out of BigQuery.

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:11.340
You could just send them
flat out to Cloud Storage,

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:13.634
or you could send
them to Pub/Sub.

00:19:13.634 --> 00:19:16.050
Those two queues cases are
interesting because those of us

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:19.320
who are operating another type
of logging infrastructure,

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:22.200
whether you're operating
Splunk or something like that,

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:24.000
or you have a SIM.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:25.530
Your SoC is leveraging
a SIM and you

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:27.269
want to basically
say, no, I have

00:19:27.269 --> 00:19:28.560
several different environments.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:30.240
I want everything
to go into that SIM.

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:33.240
This is where you would
pivot that information.

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:35.040
You would say, OK,
drop it into a bucket

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:36.270
and I'll set up a
bucket trigger and I'll

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:38.061
know to basically pull
that information in.

00:19:38.061 --> 00:19:39.990
In the case of
Splunk and others,

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:41.820
if you send it to
Pub/Sub, they're

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:44.920
able to hook into that Pub/Sub
feed and bring things over.

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:48.460
But the point is-- the
objective is-- to move it out.

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:50.910
What does look like from
a technical perspective?

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:53.850
We do something called
setting up syncs,

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:56.730
and the sync just basically
says, hey, Stackdriver,

00:19:56.730 --> 00:20:01.300
I know the data is inside
of the Stackdriver platform.

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:01.890
I want it out.

00:20:01.890 --> 00:20:07.300
And so you can, in a very
terse amount of language,

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:09.300
you can say what is the
log that I'm interesting

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:11.580
in-- essentially
set up the filter--

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:13.196
and where do I want it to go.

00:20:13.196 --> 00:20:14.820
What Stackdriver will
do is turn around

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:17.230
and say, OK, I'll go ahead
and create that for you.

00:20:17.230 --> 00:20:19.770
Here's a specific service
account within your environment

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:21.324
that's going to be
used for the sync.

00:20:21.324 --> 00:20:23.490
And go ahead and if you've
asked for it in this case

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:25.710
to go to BigQuery, notice
these are not necessarily

00:20:25.710 --> 00:20:28.126
in the same project because
we have our forensics project,

00:20:28.126 --> 00:20:30.879
we have our security project
where all this data is going.

00:20:30.879 --> 00:20:33.170
And so it's important to turn
around and go to the data

00:20:33.170 --> 00:20:34.800
set or the bucket
or wherever you're

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:37.020
destining this
information and say, oh, I

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:39.735
need to take that service
account that Stackdriver told

00:20:39.735 --> 00:20:41.110
me it was going
to use and I need

00:20:41.110 --> 00:20:42.693
to grant it the
appropriate permission

00:20:42.693 --> 00:20:44.290
to be able to write to that.

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:46.140
So this is great.

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:49.041
If you do this, you've got
this kind of automated.

00:20:49.041 --> 00:20:50.040
Every instance comes up.

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:51.390
You make it part of
the bootstrap process

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:53.348
that every instance is
going to be able to send

00:20:53.348 --> 00:20:55.760
this information off.

00:20:55.760 --> 00:20:57.980
But this test tells
us syslog information.

00:20:57.980 --> 00:21:00.690
Syslog information is great
in terms of standard system

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:01.190
activities.

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:04.564
It's not perfectly useful if
you want to go much deeper.

00:21:04.564 --> 00:21:06.230
If you want to go at
the forensic level,

00:21:06.230 --> 00:21:08.660
we need to look at, OK,
what is this instance doing?

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:11.810
What are the system calls
that are being made?

00:21:11.810 --> 00:21:13.980
Who's asking for different
things to happen?

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.490
So we need to go and upgrade
the amount of information

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:19.378
we're getting from an instance.

00:21:19.378 --> 00:21:22.600
So within Linux,
there's something

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:25.030
called the Linux
auditing system, which

00:21:25.030 --> 00:21:26.320
is something you can tap into.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:29.860
Every single activity-- there's
something like 300-odd sys

00:21:29.860 --> 00:21:31.990
calls that are being
done within Linux,

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:34.852
but you can look at
every single activity.

00:21:34.852 --> 00:21:36.310
You can look at
every time somebody

00:21:36.310 --> 00:21:38.150
touched a certain file,
read a certain file.

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:41.280
You can look at when somebody
has asked to open a socket

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:43.870
or listen on a socket.

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:47.230
So this is when somebody
has asked to run something

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:48.454
with privileged information.

00:21:48.454 --> 00:21:50.620
This information is actually
there and being emitted

00:21:50.620 --> 00:21:52.360
from the kernel all the time.

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:54.850
There's a mechanism where
the kernel sends it over

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:58.626
to the user space for some
tool to do what with it--

00:21:58.626 --> 00:22:00.250
the kernel doesn't
really dictate that.

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:03.142
It uses something called
a netlink socket and says,

00:22:03.142 --> 00:22:05.350
I'm just going to dump this
as a flood of information

00:22:05.350 --> 00:22:06.302
out on this.

00:22:06.302 --> 00:22:08.260
And so you can have things
that listen for that

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:09.160
and pull it off.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:10.630
Auditd is one such tool.

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:13.210
Another such tool
would be osquery.

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:16.670
The tool is really sort of your
preference and your choice,

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:18.670
but the point is, you
need something in userland

00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:22.000
to basically subscribe to that
and take that off and then send

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.340
it, in this case, to logging.

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:27.280
So we need to instrument that
a couple of different ways,

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:29.926
which I'll show in a
second, but in this case,

00:22:29.926 --> 00:22:32.050
every time somebody has
done something privileged--

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:34.216
in this case, somebody has
done something using sudo

00:22:34.216 --> 00:22:35.895
and we're interested in that--

00:22:35.895 --> 00:22:37.270
that goes to
Stackdriver Logging.

00:22:37.270 --> 00:22:41.050
Because we've pre-filled out
the collection part of this

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:43.680
with an agent, then essentially
we just need to say,

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:45.597
yep, this is one more
thing I wanted to learn.

00:22:45.597 --> 00:22:46.971
Without this kind
of information,

00:22:46.971 --> 00:22:48.400
it's very, very
hard to really get

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:49.816
a picture of what
the instance was

00:22:49.816 --> 00:22:51.700
doing-- what the VM was doing.

00:22:51.700 --> 00:22:53.680
You'll sort of maybe
see some startup times,

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:56.200
some application launch times,
but without actually looking

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:59.207
at kernel-level activities
or audit-level activities,

00:22:59.207 --> 00:23:01.540
you may not necessarily know,
like, who did what in what

00:23:01.540 --> 00:23:04.090
sense.

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:09.610
Getting this onto an instance
is pretty straightforward,

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:11.170
pretty trivial.

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:13.060
If you think about
the previous slides,

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:15.130
we showed, hey, you would
have a startup script.

00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:16.900
Install the Stackdriver
Logging agent.

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:17.530
Great.

00:23:17.530 --> 00:23:19.587
Pretty much with
one command, you'd

00:23:19.587 --> 00:23:20.920
get all the Stackdriver Logging.

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:23.769
It's about the same here.

00:23:23.769 --> 00:23:25.810
Well I guess there's a
few more than one command.

00:23:25.810 --> 00:23:27.710
It's like a long command.

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:30.440
So there's a couple of
different things here.

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:32.050
One is we already
sort of acknowledged

00:23:32.050 --> 00:23:33.966
that we're going to have
a Stackdriver Logging

00:23:33.966 --> 00:23:38.290
agent ready to go, but we're
going to pull down auditd

00:23:38.290 --> 00:23:40.010
and we need to do
a couple of things.

00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:42.420
One is we need to-- in
that top little block

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:44.920
in the audit rules,
that's where we tell

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:47.920
auditd what am I interested in.

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:50.380
So this is about the version
that would fit on a slide.

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:52.370
It's not necessarily
meant to be prescriptive.

00:23:52.370 --> 00:23:54.520
You'd want to do some
research on auditd

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:56.530
and see what kind of
calls are meaningful to be

00:23:56.530 --> 00:23:57.232
able to look at.

00:23:57.232 --> 00:23:58.690
In this case, we're
saying, tell me

00:23:58.690 --> 00:24:00.795
everything that has
an effective UID of 0.

00:24:00.795 --> 00:24:02.170
Like, basically
tell me anything,

00:24:02.170 --> 00:24:03.920
like on the previous
slide, that was being

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:05.969
executed with sudo or as root.

00:24:05.969 --> 00:24:08.260
The other thing we need to
do-- the back half of that--

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:10.260
is great, we're generating
all this information.

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:11.800
It's going into audit log.

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:13.830
Stackdriver by
default doesn't know

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:15.580
that this is a source
of logging that we'd

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:18.700
like to also include with the
logs that come off of this box.

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:20.660
So that end piece
there that says--

00:24:20.660 --> 00:24:23.130
is a configuration that
says, hey, Stackdriver,

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:25.630
go ahead and pay attention
to this new log format

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:26.760
that I've dropped out.

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:28.510
And by the way, it's
a little bit special.

00:24:28.510 --> 00:24:30.030
It uses some key value pairs.

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:32.950
It has to be parsed a certain
way in order to make it in.

00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:36.030
So that's what that
format key value is.

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:37.264
But if you do this, great.

00:24:37.264 --> 00:24:39.430
You've got everything that's
happening from a syslog

00:24:39.430 --> 00:24:39.980
perspective.

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:41.646
You've got everything
down to everything

00:24:41.646 --> 00:24:43.820
that the kernel
is thinking about.

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:45.670
So in terms of
logging positions,

00:24:45.670 --> 00:24:47.020
we've got our platform logs.

00:24:47.020 --> 00:24:49.720
We've got our data access logs.

00:24:49.720 --> 00:24:52.662
We've got our OS logs, our
application server logs.

00:24:52.662 --> 00:24:54.870
We've got everything that's
happening on the instance

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:57.130
and now we know what's
going down to the kernel.

00:24:57.130 --> 00:24:59.860
The last kind of
piece of this is--

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:02.500
from a logging perspective--

00:25:02.500 --> 00:25:04.790
is flow logs.

00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:07.622
Flow logs essentially tell you--

00:25:07.622 --> 00:25:09.580
I've just finished
describing everything that's

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:10.872
going on inside of an instance.

00:25:10.872 --> 00:25:13.121
Everything that's going on
from a platform perspective

00:25:13.121 --> 00:25:14.000
around an instance.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:15.310
This sort of rounds
out the piece.

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:17.518
So tell me everything that's
happening from a network

00:25:17.518 --> 00:25:18.370
perspective.

00:25:18.370 --> 00:25:23.380
Everything that's from a
network passing-- and I

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:26.110
think somebody mentioned looking
for spurious network patterns--

00:25:26.110 --> 00:25:27.700
everything that's
being thrown back

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:30.690
and forth across our instances.

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:33.610
And this is not on by default.
This is pretty chatty.

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:34.510
It's a lot of data.

00:25:34.510 --> 00:25:36.230
It's a lot of traffic.

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:38.716
But it's something
that you would

00:25:38.716 --> 00:25:39.840
want to look at turning on.

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:41.923
So you would go in your
subnet and you'd basically

00:25:41.923 --> 00:25:43.770
say "turn flow logs
on" and it's going

00:25:43.770 --> 00:25:47.430
to score every TCP and UDP
flow, with some caveats.

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:49.240
If it's a single
packet type thing--

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:52.020
it's based on sampling, so
in some sampling senses,

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:56.310
it may not pick up on very, very
low packet-- like single packet

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:59.120
count type things-- but if
you've got something that's

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:02.550
steadying a stream out, which
is what a lot of attacks do,

00:26:02.550 --> 00:26:06.490
this would be a good
way to pick up on it.

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:08.250
And because it's in
Stackdriver Logging,

00:26:08.250 --> 00:26:11.970
I can formulate
this search pattern.

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:14.320
Now here, obviously
everybody recognizes this.

00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:17.670
This is a great DNS
server to be using.

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:19.300
This is Google's DNS server.

00:26:19.300 --> 00:26:21.697
So this isn't
necessarily suspicious.

00:26:21.697 --> 00:26:24.030
What would be suspicious,
though, is in your environment

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:28.470
if you're like, why do I have
any outbound DNS traffic?

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:31.110
I have centralized DNS
within my environment.

00:26:31.110 --> 00:26:33.240
Instances kind of look inward.

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:37.350
Why am I sending DNS
traffic out to the internet?

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:39.270
And there are some
exploits that are actually

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:42.180
pretty well-known now that
take advantage of exactly that

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:44.067
using DNS tunneling or DNScat.

00:26:44.067 --> 00:26:45.900
They're able to sort
of compromise a machine

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:47.639
and say, make it look
like a DNS query,

00:26:47.639 --> 00:26:49.680
but every time you make
the query, just piggyback

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:52.560
on this little tidbit
of information.

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:54.160
And over time, that
information gets

00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:55.410
accumulated on the other side.

00:26:55.410 --> 00:26:57.570
So this may be a very
interesting query,

00:26:57.570 --> 00:27:01.020
not necessarily going to Google
but going out to somewhere

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:02.580
that you didn't expect.

00:27:02.580 --> 00:27:03.450
All right.

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:05.631
That is the entirety
of logging--

00:27:05.631 --> 00:27:07.380
what the platform is
doing, what your data

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:10.350
is doing, what the instance
is doing, very deep,

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:11.700
and what's happening around it.

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:14.580
So that kind of rounds out our
picture of logging-- collecting

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:17.240
that information, putting
it in that logging basket,

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:18.990
and having it be
searchable because you've

00:27:18.990 --> 00:27:20.402
sent it to a
searchable endpoint.

00:27:20.402 --> 00:27:22.860
You've sent it to something
not only in Stackdriver Logging

00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:24.990
that you can query with
some advance queries,

00:27:24.990 --> 00:27:26.732
you've sent it to BigQuery.

00:27:26.732 --> 00:27:28.440
Now you'll be able to
say, you know what?

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:30.329
I think I need to
zone in on everything

00:27:30.329 --> 00:27:32.370
that was running on a
particular managed instance

00:27:32.370 --> 00:27:34.140
group or anything with
this particular tag

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:36.750
or anything with Service A.
You've got a point to basically

00:27:36.750 --> 00:27:38.190
come in and say,
great, I'm going

00:27:38.190 --> 00:27:40.320
to carve out that set
of logs and the traffic

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:41.760
patterns and the
kernel activities

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:42.990
and send that somewhere.

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:46.850
We'll talk about where
to send it in a second.

00:27:46.850 --> 00:27:47.780
Disks.

00:27:47.780 --> 00:27:52.720
So this is usually the
canonical forensic,

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:55.750
"hey, I'm going to do an
investigation" activity.

00:27:55.750 --> 00:28:00.040
Grab the disks and send it to
somebody that may or may not

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:01.270
look at them.

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:03.440
So we grab the disks
and look at it.

00:28:03.440 --> 00:28:05.570
So obviously in a
Cloud environment,

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:09.577
we don't have the
disk grab API yet,

00:28:09.577 --> 00:28:11.410
where you can tell a
human being to grab it,

00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:12.370
but that's not what you want.

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:13.910
Really what you
want is the disk.

00:28:13.910 --> 00:28:15.371
And if you remember the don'ts--

00:28:15.371 --> 00:28:17.620
the things not to do-- is
do this in an obtrusive way.

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:20.200
We don't want to do this in
a way that reveals that we're

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:23.470
doing something investigatory.

00:28:23.470 --> 00:28:25.229
Logs happen saliently.

00:28:25.229 --> 00:28:26.770
All those agents,
as long as you make

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:30.424
those part of your build-up,
your bootstrap process,

00:28:30.424 --> 00:28:32.590
all that information is
spewing and nobody is really

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:34.840
the wiser from an attacker
perspective of how much

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:36.880
information is being sent off.

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:39.300
I mean, they probably
perceivably look,

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:42.110
but you're not instrumenting
something after their attack.

00:28:42.110 --> 00:28:44.740
It's just part of
the normal process.

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:45.850
Disks are the same way.

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:47.950
We don't want to terminate disks
and say, oh no, but it's OK.

00:28:47.950 --> 00:28:49.280
I just had to save the disk.

00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:51.940
We want a much more salient
way to instrument this.

00:28:51.940 --> 00:28:56.260
We want to use Cloud APIs to
do this a little bit better.

00:28:56.260 --> 00:28:59.500
So what this process would
look like, or does look like,

00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:03.370
is to enumerate the
instance or instances

00:29:03.370 --> 00:29:04.900
that you are interested in.

00:29:04.900 --> 00:29:08.710
OK, Service A is compromised or
Microservice B is compromised.

00:29:08.710 --> 00:29:12.160
What are all the instances
that are running that?

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:15.130
Come up with a list of disks
attached to those instances,

00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:17.880
and behind the scenes, use
a platform to make a copy.

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.207
And those copies
exist in logical form

00:29:20.207 --> 00:29:23.180
from an object perspective
within your Cloud account.

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:24.850
What we want is a
tangible artifact

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:26.980
that can be sent to
a downstream tool.

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:28.490
So what does that look like?

00:29:28.490 --> 00:29:29.906
It's actually just
three commands.

00:29:31.960 --> 00:29:35.560
It is possible to take a
running disk that's attached

00:29:35.560 --> 00:29:38.344
and make a snapshot from it.

00:29:38.344 --> 00:29:40.510
The reason to make a snapshot
is because these disks

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:41.509
are attached.

00:29:41.509 --> 00:29:43.300
If you wanted to make--
it's a prerequisite

00:29:43.300 --> 00:29:45.580
for the next step, which
is to create a disk

00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:47.290
image from a source.

00:29:47.290 --> 00:29:52.810
So you can create a disk
image from a persistent disk,

00:29:52.810 --> 00:29:55.580
but if that persistent disk
is attached, we can't do it.

00:29:55.580 --> 00:29:57.930
So first we take a
snapshot, we create

00:29:57.930 --> 00:30:01.360
a point in time of a
disk that's attached,

00:30:01.360 --> 00:30:04.086
we create a disk image
from that snapshot,

00:30:04.086 --> 00:30:05.710
and then we can export
that disk image.

00:30:05.710 --> 00:30:09.627
Now this may be something
interesting just from your own

00:30:09.627 --> 00:30:11.710
operational perspective--
how do I get information

00:30:11.710 --> 00:30:13.180
that's running--

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:15.620
how do I get a disk image
that's running on production

00:30:15.620 --> 00:30:17.710
and have a backup
copy in case I need

00:30:17.710 --> 00:30:20.089
to change environments or
do something interesting?

00:30:20.089 --> 00:30:21.880
But this is incredibly
effective because we

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:23.994
can create an entire
external archive

00:30:23.994 --> 00:30:25.660
in the form of a file
that can be pulled

00:30:25.660 --> 00:30:27.520
into another tool for analysis.

00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:29.380
Again, an attacker
that's running something

00:30:29.380 --> 00:30:31.270
on an instance you're
running has no idea

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:32.540
that this activity took place.

00:30:35.840 --> 00:30:37.050
OK.

00:30:37.050 --> 00:30:37.990
That was it for disks.

00:30:37.990 --> 00:30:39.770
It's really, really
straightforward and simple.

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:41.610
It's one of the biggest
things that you'll

00:30:41.610 --> 00:30:44.580
do in your analysis activity.

00:30:44.580 --> 00:30:47.880
But I'd like to move on to the
last part of the puzzle, which

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:49.890
is-- the last part of
the things we collect,

00:30:49.890 --> 00:30:52.350
which is live information.

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:54.602
Again, we need to talk to
environments that are up.

00:30:54.602 --> 00:30:56.310
And so we're sort of
done with the things

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:58.830
that the platform and some
agents and bootstrapping

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:00.192
can do.

00:31:00.192 --> 00:31:02.650
We're going to move on to,
like, how do I look at something

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:03.990
that is going on in the box?

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:05.340
You've been informed
there's a compromise.

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:07.340
You know there's an
instance that's compromised.

00:31:07.340 --> 00:31:08.250
I want to log in.

00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:11.002
I can't log in because that
would give my credentials away.

00:31:11.002 --> 00:31:12.960
But clearly there's
something running in memory

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:14.880
on that box that's
sending data out.

00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:17.260
So GRR is an agent-based system.

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:20.370
It actually installs on
Windows, MacOS, Linux.

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:23.920
It's an open-source project that
started at Google a while ago.

00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:30.120
It's actually GRR
Rapid Response.

00:31:30.120 --> 00:31:32.616
To be cool, you say "grr."

00:31:32.616 --> 00:31:35.900
I was talking with some of
the folks that contribute

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:38.570
and create to it and
I kept saying G-R-R

00:31:38.570 --> 00:31:41.059
and they said that
I wasn't cool enough

00:31:41.059 --> 00:31:42.850
to give the talk because
I don't say "grr."

00:31:42.850 --> 00:31:46.506
So say "grr" to be cool.

00:31:46.506 --> 00:31:49.161
GRR know-before-you-go.

00:31:49.161 --> 00:31:50.510
With great power comes--

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:52.760
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:52.760 --> 00:31:54.500
SAMI ZUHURUDDIN:
No, that's wrong.

00:31:54.500 --> 00:31:58.460
So it's a lot of
extensive capability.

00:31:58.460 --> 00:32:00.920
It can let you do
literally anything.

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:05.660
And so it is that sharp
end of the spear upon which

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:08.570
you can do pretty much anything
inside your environment,

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:13.400
so it's probably a good idea
to fortify the heck out of it.

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:15.470
Effectively, it is
the point at which

00:32:15.470 --> 00:32:19.050
you could be root on anything
and look at anything.

00:32:19.050 --> 00:32:22.036
So there is an entire--

00:32:22.036 --> 00:32:24.930
in the GRR readthedocs
there's an entire section

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:26.190
on how to secure it.

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:27.610
You know, how do
you front-end it,

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:29.490
how do you put the
right certificates on.

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:31.380
That would be an
entire talk on its own.

00:32:31.380 --> 00:32:34.680
This is just my way to make
sure that Google security

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:37.180
people don't yell at me.

00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:39.800
But secure it
before you move on.

00:32:39.800 --> 00:32:41.520
OK.

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:43.440
Once you've got
GRR up and running,

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:45.085
there are two main activities--

00:32:45.085 --> 00:32:46.710
clients will report
into it but there's

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:49.590
two sort of main things
you would ask GRR to do,

00:32:49.590 --> 00:32:50.940
a flow or a hunt.

00:32:50.940 --> 00:32:53.940
And so a flow is just
an atomic unit of work.

00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:55.950
You're like, hey,
that box right there,

00:32:55.950 --> 00:32:57.907
I'd like to look at
a particular file.

00:32:57.907 --> 00:32:59.490
That box right there,
I'd like to look

00:32:59.490 --> 00:33:02.110
at the memory that's running
on that particular process.

00:33:02.110 --> 00:33:03.840
So GRR can essentially
start a flow

00:33:03.840 --> 00:33:05.760
and say, great, who
do you want to target?

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:07.474
What do you want to
do on that machine?

00:33:07.474 --> 00:33:10.140
And then that machine would pull
in and ask if there's any work.

00:33:10.140 --> 00:33:11.910
A hunt would be sort of
under this new banner

00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:12.660
of threat hunting.

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:15.505
If you just sort of
go from a premise

00:33:15.505 --> 00:33:17.130
that you're always
compromised, there's

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:19.410
something always going
on in your environment,

00:33:19.410 --> 00:33:20.850
then you may say,
you know, I know

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:23.190
that there's this shape of
this jar that's pretty bad

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:24.450
or I know there's
this string that's

00:33:24.450 --> 00:33:25.890
pretty indicative
of a compromise

00:33:25.890 --> 00:33:28.061
and I want to be hunting
my fleet for any time that

00:33:28.061 --> 00:33:28.560
shows up.

00:33:31.500 --> 00:33:33.779
So this is an example
of a GRR flow.

00:33:33.779 --> 00:33:35.320
I've asked for a
process memory dump.

00:33:35.320 --> 00:33:37.236
So let's say, for example,
you have a machine.

00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:42.340
An attacker's on the machine
and says, you know, rudimentary,

00:33:42.340 --> 00:33:44.170
I'm just going to
actually take this file.

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:48.010
I'm going to dump the
entire creditcards.zip--

00:33:48.010 --> 00:33:50.231
no, don't ever do that.

00:33:50.231 --> 00:33:51.730
I'm going to take
that and basically

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:53.449
make it look like
some system-level file

00:33:53.449 --> 00:33:55.240
because I've made it
look like a swap file,

00:33:55.240 --> 00:33:57.490
and I'm going to just
send it over a socket.

00:33:57.490 --> 00:34:00.447
I've only got 20
minutes on this box.

00:34:00.447 --> 00:34:01.780
And so you could send this over.

00:34:01.780 --> 00:34:03.196
And so now you've
got this process

00:34:03.196 --> 00:34:04.599
which is online,
which is active,

00:34:04.599 --> 00:34:05.890
which is egressing information.

00:34:05.890 --> 00:34:08.840
You're like, hey, that
doesn't look right.

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:11.230
So you start a flow
and say, I would

00:34:11.230 --> 00:34:14.770
like to dump that process.

00:34:14.770 --> 00:34:16.420
So GRR will turn
around, and then

00:34:16.420 --> 00:34:18.250
when that box makes contacts,
it will say, hey, turn around

00:34:18.250 --> 00:34:19.120
and run this flow.

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:22.510
And so you'll see in the GRR
console, it's like, OK, cool.

00:34:22.510 --> 00:34:25.170
I brought this piece of work
here you've asked me to do.

00:34:25.170 --> 00:34:27.670
And it will go on and collect,
and then a few moments later,

00:34:27.670 --> 00:34:30.147
you'll see that, hey,
GRR, say I'm done.

00:34:30.147 --> 00:34:31.480
I'm done collecting this memory.

00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:33.909
I've stored in these
however many little artifact

00:34:33.909 --> 00:34:35.132
files-- these files.

00:34:35.132 --> 00:34:36.590
You're ready to
come check it out--

00:34:36.590 --> 00:34:38.714
sort of like your dry
cleaning is ready to pick up.

00:34:38.714 --> 00:34:40.780
And so you can pull
those files down.

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:43.604
And they're files that represent
the binary representation

00:34:43.604 --> 00:34:45.520
of what's in memory, so
you could do something

00:34:45.520 --> 00:34:46.870
as simple as run
strings on it, which

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:48.286
would take that
binary information

00:34:48.286 --> 00:34:50.560
and turn it back
into text, and you

00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:53.290
could see that essentially
what I had sent was sitting--

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:55.750
whatever you had been
sending was sitting resident

00:34:55.750 --> 00:34:57.370
in memory for that
process still.

00:34:57.370 --> 00:35:01.090
It hadn't been
garbage collected yet.

00:35:01.090 --> 00:35:02.850
And you would be able
to dump that out.

00:35:02.850 --> 00:35:04.640
And so it's pretty powerful
to be able to say, look,

00:35:04.640 --> 00:35:06.370
I can actually look and
reach inside of a box

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:08.536
and look at the memory
that's going on inside of it.

00:35:08.536 --> 00:35:10.870
It's not necessarily
going to be this easy.

00:35:10.870 --> 00:35:12.149
This is trivialized.

00:35:12.149 --> 00:35:13.690
But the point I'm
trying to make here

00:35:13.690 --> 00:35:15.895
is, without GRR, you
couldn't do this.

00:35:15.895 --> 00:35:17.020
Remember, you're hamstrung.

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:19.999
Your hands are tied behind your
back because you can't log in.

00:35:19.999 --> 00:35:21.040
You don't want to log in.

00:35:21.040 --> 00:35:23.830
You know that's
just a bad practice.

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:26.290
But with GRR, at least
you can effectively

00:35:26.290 --> 00:35:31.800
do everything you would need
to do had you been logged in.

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:34.840
GRR actually is designed to
go onto agents, like Windows

00:35:34.840 --> 00:35:37.510
machines, MacOS machines,
machines that are out

00:35:37.510 --> 00:35:39.700
on the internet-- the
agents are available--

00:35:39.700 --> 00:35:44.385
and be contacted from, say,
a fleet of workstations

00:35:44.385 --> 00:35:45.260
that you're managing.

00:35:45.260 --> 00:35:46.960
But in a cloud deployment
here, for using

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:48.250
in this sort of
centralized context

00:35:48.250 --> 00:35:50.410
where you're having
machines-- instances spin up

00:35:50.410 --> 00:35:52.540
in different projects
and contact in,

00:35:52.540 --> 00:35:54.790
we'd want to make it work
in this sort of, again,

00:35:54.790 --> 00:35:57.400
segmented approach, where not
everybody has access to the GRR

00:35:57.400 --> 00:35:57.950
server.

00:35:57.950 --> 00:35:59.890
It is a pull model,
so the nice thing,

00:35:59.890 --> 00:36:01.999
it's not GRR reaching
out and making contacts

00:36:01.999 --> 00:36:03.540
and you having to
deal with firewalls

00:36:03.540 --> 00:36:05.500
in every single instance.

00:36:05.500 --> 00:36:08.780
Agents on
client-installed machines

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:10.072
pull periodically for activity.

00:36:10.072 --> 00:36:12.071
And the nice thing is,
then, if they're offline,

00:36:12.071 --> 00:36:15.130
they're offline, but when they
come online, they'll say, hey,

00:36:15.130 --> 00:36:16.880
do you have any work for me?

00:36:16.880 --> 00:36:19.540
And the other thing
GRR does is it has

00:36:19.540 --> 00:36:20.920
customized install binaries.

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:23.510
And so you'll put those
binaries in a specific bucket

00:36:23.510 --> 00:36:25.630
and you'll set up your
internal firewall rules.

00:36:25.630 --> 00:36:28.000
But the way to do this sort of
cross-project-- because again,

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:30.041
we want our security
artifacts and infrastructure

00:36:30.041 --> 00:36:32.890
in a different project
than our production ones--

00:36:32.890 --> 00:36:36.130
we'd want to set up
VPC peering here.

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:40.330
So this isn't really that
much of an interesting slide,

00:36:40.330 --> 00:36:42.070
but essentially,
it's a few steps.

00:36:42.070 --> 00:36:45.262
You can set up peering between
two pieces of infrastructure.

00:36:45.262 --> 00:36:46.720
And the main thing
there is to make

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:50.230
sure you allow the relevant
protocols into your GRR server.

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:53.019
The GRR server itself is
pretty easy to set up.

00:36:53.019 --> 00:36:54.060
It's got Debian packages.

00:36:54.060 --> 00:36:55.410
It comes with Docker--

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:57.220
I mean, you can
download a Docker image

00:36:57.220 --> 00:36:58.600
and run a Docker image.

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:01.570
Part of the setup will
interrogate you and ask you,

00:37:01.570 --> 00:37:03.400
what is the name
by which clients

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:04.720
are going to contact me?

00:37:04.720 --> 00:37:06.649
And you configure all
that information in.

00:37:06.649 --> 00:37:08.440
What is the admin
interface going to be on?

00:37:08.440 --> 00:37:09.856
You configure all
that information

00:37:09.856 --> 00:37:12.490
in as part of the setup, and
then as it's doing the setup,

00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:14.800
it will build custom
install binaries.

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:17.380
So it isn't something--
and this is really salient

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:20.440
because then these install
binaries are just ready to go.

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:21.820
So you'd put those
into a bucket.

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:25.240
That's basically where they
sit on the management console.

00:37:25.240 --> 00:37:27.365
And what you want to
do, then, is any piece

00:37:27.365 --> 00:37:28.990
of infrastructure in
your environment--

00:37:28.990 --> 00:37:31.156
any compute that needs to
grab those binaries-- just

00:37:31.156 --> 00:37:33.797
make sure you give those
access to the bucket.

00:37:33.797 --> 00:37:34.380
And that's it.

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:37.270
It's sort of a one pull-- you
know, pull down the archive

00:37:37.270 --> 00:37:40.454
from a bucket using gsutil
and then install it.

00:37:40.454 --> 00:37:42.370
So now we've kind of
talked about three things

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:43.328
in our install script--

00:37:43.328 --> 00:37:47.440
Stackdriver Logging agent,
auditd, and now the GRR agent.

00:37:47.440 --> 00:37:49.570
With this, we've got
our logging information,

00:37:49.570 --> 00:37:51.280
we've talked about
disk collection,

00:37:51.280 --> 00:37:55.300
and we've talked about
getting information

00:37:55.300 --> 00:37:56.390
using live information.

00:37:56.390 --> 00:37:57.670
So let's move on to detection.

00:37:57.670 --> 00:37:59.503
This is kind of where
it starts to get real.

00:37:59.503 --> 00:38:00.930
We're in a good position here.

00:38:00.930 --> 00:38:02.680
We're in a position
that if somebody said,

00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:04.846
tell me everything that
goes on in your environment,

00:38:04.846 --> 00:38:08.260
and you, instead of
looking at them like a deer

00:38:08.260 --> 00:38:10.600
in headlights, which
is easy to do up here

00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:12.940
with lights in your face--

00:38:12.940 --> 00:38:14.947
instead of that, you
can go, I got this.

00:38:14.947 --> 00:38:16.030
I can pull a disk for you.

00:38:16.030 --> 00:38:17.530
I can tell you what's
going on in memory.

00:38:17.530 --> 00:38:19.270
I can pull logs related
to that incident.

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:21.616
I can bundle this all
together and hand it over.

00:38:21.616 --> 00:38:23.350
And so, in detecting
this, this is

00:38:23.350 --> 00:38:27.105
where you would employ
any number of services.

00:38:27.105 --> 00:38:28.480
Maybe you're
hunting for malware.

00:38:28.480 --> 00:38:30.550
Maybe you're noticing that your
instances are beaconing out

00:38:30.550 --> 00:38:32.380
to some-- you know, trying to
make contact with the command

00:38:32.380 --> 00:38:33.130
or control server.

00:38:33.130 --> 00:38:35.350
Your IDS pro picks it up.

00:38:35.350 --> 00:38:37.540
Unexpected processes
was called out earlier

00:38:37.540 --> 00:38:38.790
on unauthorized sys calls.

00:38:38.790 --> 00:38:43.550
Why is something trying
to listen on a port?

00:38:43.550 --> 00:38:46.430
So let's say you put
that all together.

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:49.270
This is an example of
what a pipeline on Cloud

00:38:49.270 --> 00:38:50.130
could look like--

00:38:50.130 --> 00:38:51.010
a forensics pipeline.

00:38:51.010 --> 00:38:51.850
If you remember
in the beginning,

00:38:51.850 --> 00:38:54.074
I said we'd sort of pull
all of this together

00:38:54.074 --> 00:38:55.240
to make it look interesting.

00:38:55.240 --> 00:38:58.310
But imagine you have an
instance that's beaconing.

00:38:58.310 --> 00:39:02.260
It's sending out to
some bad known IP.

00:39:02.260 --> 00:39:06.820
So before the fact, you've sort
of sourced a list of bad IPs.

00:39:06.820 --> 00:39:08.420
We'll talk about
that in a second.

00:39:08.420 --> 00:39:10.628
And so you've got the machine
beaconing, which you're

00:39:10.628 --> 00:39:13.090
able to find out because
that's going into Flow Logs,

00:39:13.090 --> 00:39:15.929
and you've got a list of bad
IPs that you've configured

00:39:15.929 --> 00:39:17.470
as a filter because
you're like, hey,

00:39:17.470 --> 00:39:19.750
I can configure a filter
to look for certain IPs.

00:39:19.750 --> 00:39:21.970
And if you notice
that, you could

00:39:21.970 --> 00:39:24.490
tell some worker process
that says, you know,

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:25.906
this doesn't look right.

00:39:25.906 --> 00:39:27.280
I want you to
trigger off of that

00:39:27.280 --> 00:39:29.071
and I want you to go
and collect everything

00:39:29.071 --> 00:39:30.485
you know about that instance.

00:39:30.485 --> 00:39:32.110
And because Flow Logs
are fairly chatty

00:39:32.110 --> 00:39:33.340
and that would keep
repeating, you'd probably

00:39:33.340 --> 00:39:34.756
checkpoint these
results somewhere

00:39:34.756 --> 00:39:37.810
and say, oh, this is
instance demo one.

00:39:37.810 --> 00:39:38.740
It's been compromised.

00:39:38.740 --> 00:39:40.280
You don't want to kick
off this flow every time,

00:39:40.280 --> 00:39:42.550
so you'd maybe collect that
information in Firestore,

00:39:42.550 --> 00:39:45.070
and Firestore has a few
different ways to use it.

00:39:45.070 --> 00:39:47.030
The way we're talking
about here is closed.

00:39:47.030 --> 00:39:50.530
You don't want Firestore
open to mobile and web.

00:39:50.530 --> 00:39:54.760
You know, this is just
basically a holding ground for--

00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:55.770
it's not a social app.

00:39:55.770 --> 00:39:57.700
It's just a holding ground
to checkpoint your results.

00:39:57.700 --> 00:39:59.658
And so I would turn around
and I would do that.

00:39:59.658 --> 00:40:03.080
I would say, collect me
the memory, grab the disks,

00:40:03.080 --> 00:40:05.480
run this query to collect all
the logs for that instance,

00:40:05.480 --> 00:40:07.220
then send those
to the next stage.

00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:09.290
And the next stage would
be, I've got the memory.

00:40:09.290 --> 00:40:10.280
What do you want me to do?

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:12.613
Kick off another process which
turns around and analyzes

00:40:12.613 --> 00:40:14.596
that with Volatility
or Rekall or something.

00:40:14.596 --> 00:40:16.470
And I've got all these
time-based artifacts--

00:40:16.470 --> 00:40:17.240
I've got disks.

00:40:17.240 --> 00:40:17.900
I've got logs.

00:40:17.900 --> 00:40:20.420
I want to put those together
in a way I can look at them.

00:40:20.420 --> 00:40:22.580
You create what's called
a super timeline, which

00:40:22.580 --> 00:40:25.430
is using some tools called
Plaso and Timesketch--

00:40:25.430 --> 00:40:27.920
and oh, by the way, as
this activity was running,

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:29.622
one feature of
Stackdriver Logging

00:40:29.622 --> 00:40:30.830
is that you can use alerting.

00:40:30.830 --> 00:40:34.310
And alerting can then not only
alert based on some metric,

00:40:34.310 --> 00:40:36.849
it can do notifications with
PagerDuty and other things.

00:40:36.849 --> 00:40:38.890
And so your incident
response team gets an alert.

00:40:38.890 --> 00:40:40.790
It comes in and says,
hey, this bucket

00:40:40.790 --> 00:40:42.500
has a bunch of
artifacts, or these VMs

00:40:42.500 --> 00:40:44.090
have a bunch of artifacts
that have been loaded up

00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:45.089
in their analysis tools.

00:40:45.089 --> 00:40:47.790
They need your eyeballs
on them right away.

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:49.334
So this is not
fictitious, actually.

00:40:49.334 --> 00:40:50.750
You can build this
with everything

00:40:50.750 --> 00:40:53.240
we've already talked about
and a lot of the things

00:40:53.240 --> 00:40:54.990
we've already shown,
but I'll kind of just

00:40:54.990 --> 00:40:58.120
allude to the things we
haven't quite talked about.

00:40:58.120 --> 00:41:00.374
So MSP is an organization,
SANSes are organizations.

00:41:00.374 --> 00:41:01.790
These are well-known
organizations

00:41:01.790 --> 00:41:05.390
that help collate and curate
bad actor information,

00:41:05.390 --> 00:41:07.730
malware information, and
so they do have the ability

00:41:07.730 --> 00:41:09.460
to sort of bring down a list.

00:41:09.460 --> 00:41:11.960
It could be earlier, like we
talked about with DNS, where

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:14.820
it's maybe a particular port
that you know is troublesome,

00:41:14.820 --> 00:41:16.403
but in this case,
you could say, well,

00:41:16.403 --> 00:41:18.980
I just want to download
the top bad actor list.

00:41:18.980 --> 00:41:21.490
And because we're able to set
up syncs programmatically,

00:41:21.490 --> 00:41:23.240
like we set up earlier
a sync for archive,

00:41:23.240 --> 00:41:24.020
this is a sync that's different.

00:41:24.020 --> 00:41:26.294
You'll notice the destination
is actually Pub/Sub.

00:41:26.294 --> 00:41:28.460
And we'd set up a topic
here called, hey, beaconing.

00:41:28.460 --> 00:41:31.110
I think that if I see somebody
talking to these bad IPs,

00:41:31.110 --> 00:41:34.670
it's a pretty good indication
that they're beaconing out

00:41:34.670 --> 00:41:36.900
to make contact with someone.

00:41:36.900 --> 00:41:39.440
And so I could build this
list and sort of refresh this

00:41:39.440 --> 00:41:41.480
on a basis, and if
it triggers, boom.

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:44.769
I'm going to send it
to a Pub/Sub topic

00:41:44.769 --> 00:41:46.310
and that will kick
off the activities

00:41:46.310 --> 00:41:49.720
that you saw in
that worker chain.

00:41:49.720 --> 00:41:51.670
So this payload comes
in and that worker says,

00:41:51.670 --> 00:41:52.760
what do you want me to do?

00:41:52.760 --> 00:41:55.180
Well it turns out,
GRR has an API,

00:41:55.180 --> 00:41:57.260
and so if this was
the worker that's

00:41:57.260 --> 00:41:58.910
going to orchestrate
the GRR activity,

00:41:58.910 --> 00:42:01.330
it would say, cool, what's in
the payload of this message

00:42:01.330 --> 00:42:02.740
that I just got on Pub/Sub?

00:42:02.740 --> 00:42:04.060
And the two things that
are interesting there

00:42:04.060 --> 00:42:05.830
are the source IP and
the destination IP.

00:42:05.830 --> 00:42:09.130
So the first thing I would do
is I would contact the GRR API

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:14.650
and I would say, give me
the client that has this IP.

00:42:14.650 --> 00:42:16.660
And so GRR would search
its database and say,

00:42:16.660 --> 00:42:19.246
here's a client or
here's a list of clients.

00:42:19.246 --> 00:42:20.620
And then the next
thing, which is

00:42:20.620 --> 00:42:22.536
more interesting, I would
say, go do a netstat

00:42:22.536 --> 00:42:23.950
on that machine.

00:42:23.950 --> 00:42:28.570
Go do a netstat and tell me
in that netstat who's talking

00:42:28.570 --> 00:42:30.340
to this bad IP-- what process.

00:42:30.340 --> 00:42:32.619
Give me the PID of the process
talking to this bad IP.

00:42:32.619 --> 00:42:34.660
And then it would come
back and say, well, here's

00:42:34.660 --> 00:42:37.810
the PID of who's talking to
that IP based on the netstat.

00:42:37.810 --> 00:42:38.555
Great.

00:42:38.555 --> 00:42:40.790
Go dump me in the
memory for that PID.

00:42:40.790 --> 00:42:42.540
I want to know everything
that's happening

00:42:42.540 --> 00:42:44.340
in the memory on that PID
that's talking to that bad IP

00:42:44.340 --> 00:42:45.640
because it shouldn't be.

00:42:45.640 --> 00:42:48.180
This is all done just basically
talking and interacting

00:42:48.180 --> 00:42:49.590
with the GRR API.

00:42:49.590 --> 00:42:51.024
And it started
off with a trigger

00:42:51.024 --> 00:42:52.440
that was based on
some information

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:55.390
that we can easily source.

00:42:55.390 --> 00:42:57.220
The next thing in our
pipeline after memory

00:42:57.220 --> 00:42:58.320
was building a timeline.

00:42:58.320 --> 00:42:59.890
The timeline
essentially just says,

00:42:59.890 --> 00:43:01.540
when you can take
a bunch of data

00:43:01.540 --> 00:43:03.190
and extract the
metadata that has

00:43:03.190 --> 00:43:07.480
to do with time on that
timeline and lay it all out.

00:43:07.480 --> 00:43:10.810
You lay it all out so that
it's chronologically able

00:43:10.810 --> 00:43:13.192
to be analyzed and then
layer on anything else that

00:43:13.192 --> 00:43:14.650
has that time
element because we're

00:43:14.650 --> 00:43:17.881
sort of hunting and
finding a cataclysmic event

00:43:17.881 --> 00:43:19.630
and looking sort of
around that and around

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:21.400
that to say, ah, that's
where that file was dropped

00:43:21.400 --> 00:43:23.200
and that's where that
process was launched.

00:43:23.200 --> 00:43:25.330
That file was dropped on the
disk based on this information

00:43:25.330 --> 00:43:26.788
and that process
was launched based

00:43:26.788 --> 00:43:28.090
on this user at this time.

00:43:28.090 --> 00:43:29.627
We want all that information.

00:43:29.627 --> 00:43:31.960
So we take all our logs,
anything that's time-sensitive,

00:43:31.960 --> 00:43:32.547
and our disks.

00:43:32.547 --> 00:43:35.130
We send them to log2timeline and
it creates a super timeline--

00:43:35.130 --> 00:43:36.330
a Plaso file.

00:43:36.330 --> 00:43:38.230
A Plaso file would
just be a file that

00:43:38.230 --> 00:43:40.150
would be sort of hard
to look at and sort of

00:43:40.150 --> 00:43:41.691
like a spreadsheet
fashion, and so we

00:43:41.691 --> 00:43:44.300
want a way to sort of bring
that together and collaborate.

00:43:44.300 --> 00:43:46.712
So there's another
tool called Timesketch.

00:43:46.712 --> 00:43:48.460
Timesketch was
written by a Google

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:51.222
engineer named Johan Berggren.

00:43:51.222 --> 00:43:52.430
I hope I'm saying that right.

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:54.050
And it sort of has this--

00:43:54.050 --> 00:43:56.620
it's ElasticSearch backed
but it has this ability

00:43:56.620 --> 00:44:00.190
to load in all this timeline
information into sketches

00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:02.380
and say, hey, I want
to bring in timelines.

00:44:02.380 --> 00:44:05.207
What this does is it's a focal
point for all your analysts

00:44:05.207 --> 00:44:07.540
to sort of look at and say,
there is interesting traffic

00:44:07.540 --> 00:44:07.860
right there.

00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:08.860
I want to annotate that.

00:44:08.860 --> 00:44:10.870
I want to come back to
that in my workflow.

00:44:10.870 --> 00:44:13.540
I want to look at how that
event connects to other events.

00:44:13.540 --> 00:44:17.320
And so you can do sort of all
that collaborative exercise

00:44:17.320 --> 00:44:19.990
with Timesketch, and you can
have multiple of these going on

00:44:19.990 --> 00:44:22.365
if you have different events
going on at different times.

00:44:26.105 --> 00:44:28.230
So how do I bring things
into Plaso and Timesketch?

00:44:28.230 --> 00:44:29.520
Well I'd set up Plaso.

00:44:29.520 --> 00:44:31.330
I'd set up Timesketch.

00:44:31.330 --> 00:44:34.210
I'd copy down the disk image
after acquiring a disk image.

00:44:34.210 --> 00:44:35.395
I'd run log2timeline.

00:44:35.395 --> 00:44:37.240
I'd create a timeline.

00:44:37.240 --> 00:44:40.160
I'd basically do
a bunch of stuff.

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:42.850
And so what would be
great is if I didn't have

00:44:42.850 --> 00:44:45.060
to do all of this stuff, right?

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:47.920
If somehow, this could
all be sequenced together

00:44:47.920 --> 00:44:49.900
where I could interact
with the Cloud APIs

00:44:49.900 --> 00:44:52.360
and interact with all
these forensic analysis

00:44:52.360 --> 00:44:53.860
tools and sort of
pull it together.

00:44:53.860 --> 00:44:55.693
And so I want to highlight
a couple of tools

00:44:55.693 --> 00:44:57.910
in closing here.

00:44:57.910 --> 00:44:59.170
Is Turbinia and Timewolf.

00:44:59.170 --> 00:45:00.850
These are open-source projects.

00:45:00.850 --> 00:45:03.670
They are used by certain
teams here at Google as well,

00:45:03.670 --> 00:45:05.690
but they are available.

00:45:05.690 --> 00:45:08.020
And so Turbinia
is, if you recall,

00:45:08.020 --> 00:45:09.480
like, hey, this activity.

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:13.099
I want to create a Timesketch.

00:45:13.099 --> 00:45:14.140
I want to create a Plaso.

00:45:14.140 --> 00:45:15.937
I have this memory
in these files

00:45:15.937 --> 00:45:17.270
and I want to run strings on it.

00:45:17.270 --> 00:45:18.235
So when you have
forensic artifacts

00:45:18.235 --> 00:45:20.290
and you want to run an
activity against those forensic

00:45:20.290 --> 00:45:21.880
artifacts, this
is where you would

00:45:21.880 --> 00:45:23.557
orchestrate that with Turbinia.

00:45:23.557 --> 00:45:25.390
But you also notice in
this whole pipeline--

00:45:25.390 --> 00:45:26.889
that whole long
sequence of things--

00:45:26.889 --> 00:45:28.960
I was sequencing
between different APIs

00:45:28.960 --> 00:45:31.750
in this process of building
my forensic pipeline.

00:45:31.750 --> 00:45:34.330
And that's where a
tool like dfTimewolf

00:45:34.330 --> 00:45:37.510
comes in, where it can say it's
the plumbing or the glue that

00:45:37.510 --> 00:45:40.960
kind of pulls together disparate
actions between these APIs

00:45:40.960 --> 00:45:44.100
in the goal of being
able to kick off

00:45:44.100 --> 00:45:46.640
these more finer activities,
say, in Turbinia.

00:45:46.640 --> 00:45:49.150
So those are two tools
you can look at that lean

00:45:49.150 --> 00:45:52.270
very heavily into Cloud APIs.

00:45:52.270 --> 00:45:54.770
A question might be, hey, you've
talked about all the things

00:45:54.770 --> 00:45:56.540
in VM world.

00:45:56.540 --> 00:45:59.630
How does this pertain to
new computing environments,

00:45:59.630 --> 00:46:03.380
like containers and serverless?

00:46:03.380 --> 00:46:05.026
And a lot of the
things are applicable.

00:46:05.026 --> 00:46:06.900
In the serverless camp,
actually, very little

00:46:06.900 --> 00:46:09.060
is applicable because you don't
have control over the execution

00:46:09.060 --> 00:46:09.420
environment.

00:46:09.420 --> 00:46:11.190
You don't get the logs
from that environment.

00:46:11.190 --> 00:46:12.450
But we do get a standard out.

00:46:12.450 --> 00:46:14.991
You know, that's generally what
a serverless environment will

00:46:14.991 --> 00:46:16.890
pull out for you,
is that it will

00:46:16.890 --> 00:46:19.110
send to you whatever your
environment was sending--

00:46:19.110 --> 00:46:19.934
standard out.

00:46:19.934 --> 00:46:21.600
But in containers,
there are some things

00:46:21.600 --> 00:46:25.012
we need to keep on top of.

00:46:25.012 --> 00:46:27.350
If we're looking at
the sys call activity,

00:46:27.350 --> 00:46:29.610
there is some
utilities like Sysdig,

00:46:29.610 --> 00:46:31.010
or you could use gVisor.

00:46:31.010 --> 00:46:33.870
There's a talk tomorrow here
in this room, I think at 10:20,

00:46:33.870 --> 00:46:36.000
on gVisor, which
is, how do I look

00:46:36.000 --> 00:46:38.800
at what a container is doing?

00:46:38.800 --> 00:46:40.600
And gVisor kind of
goes a step beyond

00:46:40.600 --> 00:46:42.350
and not only looks at
what a container can

00:46:42.350 --> 00:46:44.677
do in terms of sys calls
but it can sandbox those.

00:46:44.677 --> 00:46:46.260
So you'd sort of
have a white list of,

00:46:46.260 --> 00:46:48.249
like, what should this
container be able to do?

00:46:48.249 --> 00:46:50.790
But because it's sandboxing and
intercepting those sys calls,

00:46:50.790 --> 00:46:52.166
it actually can
log off of those.

00:46:52.166 --> 00:46:54.540
So if that sounds interesting,
attend that talk tomorrow.

00:46:54.540 --> 00:46:56.370
GVisor was open-sourced
by Google recently

00:46:56.370 --> 00:46:58.717
and it's super interesting.

00:46:58.717 --> 00:47:00.300
And there's a couple
of different ways

00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:01.830
to run on Container Engine.

00:47:01.830 --> 00:47:04.800
One is using
Container-Optimized OS,

00:47:04.800 --> 00:47:06.775
and then the other is Ubuntu.

00:47:06.775 --> 00:47:09.150
And so if you want to sort of
install any of these things

00:47:09.150 --> 00:47:10.770
or work with the base
operating system,

00:47:10.770 --> 00:47:12.811
that generally means you're
going to pick Ubuntu.

00:47:14.905 --> 00:47:17.380
And then the VMs are basically
everything we talked about.

00:47:17.380 --> 00:47:20.270
But it's probably helpful to
sort of look at this compare

00:47:20.270 --> 00:47:22.190
and contrast aspect.

00:47:22.190 --> 00:47:23.780
OK.

00:47:23.780 --> 00:47:28.235
So bottom line is-- sort
of a leave-behind is that--

00:47:31.220 --> 00:47:34.070
don't go into panic
mode when something

00:47:34.070 --> 00:47:37.370
happens-- when you've been
alerted to something going on.

00:47:37.370 --> 00:47:41.390
Have a plan and
say the analysis is

00:47:41.390 --> 00:47:43.760
going to show that this
information can be found out

00:47:43.760 --> 00:47:45.710
by looking at certain
data, and that data

00:47:45.710 --> 00:47:47.887
is going to be your
logs, your disks,

00:47:47.887 --> 00:47:48.970
and your live information.

00:47:48.970 --> 00:47:52.730
And have that ready to
curate and pull together.

00:47:52.730 --> 00:47:55.010
So when something
happens, follow the plan.

00:47:55.010 --> 00:47:56.566
Gather data.

00:47:56.566 --> 00:47:58.190
Use the tools mentioned
to collaborate.

00:47:58.190 --> 00:47:59.780
It will take many
folks working together

00:47:59.780 --> 00:48:01.988
to sort of pull all the
information together and find

00:48:01.988 --> 00:48:03.115
that needle in a haystack.

00:48:03.115 --> 00:48:04.490
And then again,
be sure you bring

00:48:04.490 --> 00:48:06.140
in folks that know
what they're doing

00:48:06.140 --> 00:48:09.406
or have been trained to
know what to look for.

00:48:09.406 --> 00:48:11.780
So if the patterns in this
talk seemed interesting to you

00:48:11.780 --> 00:48:13.340
and they are some of the
things that you look to employ,

00:48:13.340 --> 00:48:15.065
we'll be probably
publishing these

00:48:15.065 --> 00:48:16.190
in the form of a solution--

00:48:16.190 --> 00:48:18.890
I work on solutions that I
publish out to the community

00:48:18.890 --> 00:48:20.840
and I'll probably try
to publish these out,

00:48:20.840 --> 00:48:23.340
but if you want to get in touch
before then and sort of talk

00:48:23.340 --> 00:48:25.220
about these patterns,
please reach out

00:48:25.220 --> 00:48:27.560
at next18-forensics@google.com.

00:48:27.560 --> 00:48:29.105
So thank you and
I hope you enjoy

00:48:29.105 --> 00:48:30.230
the rest of the conference.

00:48:30.230 --> 00:48:31.130
[APPLAUSE]

00:48:31.130 --> 00:48:34.480
